WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:04.137
-You know our next guests as Pam
and Angela from "The Office."

00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:05.572
Their book, "The Office BFFs:

00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:07.540
Tales of The Office from Two
Best Friends Who Were There,"

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:08.607
is on sale now.

00:00:08.607 --> 00:00:09.709
Please welcome to the show

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:12.478
Jenna Fischer and Angela Kinsey,
everybody!

00:00:12.478 --> 00:00:14.914
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:30.997
Welcome, you guys.
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:32.364
-Hi! It's so fun to be here.

00:00:32.364 --> 00:00:33.733
-This is usually
the time of year

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:36.168
where I would see you guys,
because you obviously shot

00:00:36.168 --> 00:00:38.904
"The Office" in L.A., but we
both had NBC shows and upfronts,

00:00:38.904 --> 00:00:40.239
which happened today.

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:43.076
You would often come out,
maybe to the "SNL" beforehand.

00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:45.311
And you had a picture of
your New York party days,

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:47.447
and it's so cool,

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:50.717
'cause it's the two of you
and B.J. Novak,

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:53.319
but it looks like it could be
the three of us.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:57.156
-Yeah, you know,
we are sober in that picture.

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:59.592
Totally sober.
-We are, I swear.

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:01.127
-Really?
-Totally.

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:02.461
-You're gonna
double down on that?

00:01:02.461 --> 00:01:05.065
-No, we were.
We love the dance floor.

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:06.832
Seth, you might remember,
at that upfronts,

00:01:06.832 --> 00:01:08.368
'cause you were there,
that Jenna and I

00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:11.504
made up a dance where
we filed papers in the cabinet.

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:12.905
-We're gonna show --
Let's do it.

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:14.440
-We did this!
-Oh, this is great.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.776
-Okay.
-Okay. It goes like this.

00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:19.111
Ready and --
-Okay.

00:01:19.111 --> 00:01:22.181
-Oh, oh, oh. Oh, I got it.

00:01:22.181 --> 00:01:23.983
-That's great. Very good.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:27.987
-Thank you.
-Very on-brand. Very on-brand.

00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:29.989
I will say, I've been to
a lot of upfronts parties,

00:01:29.989 --> 00:01:31.657
and you guys definitely did
the only dance

00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:32.858
that was thematic to your show.

00:01:32.858 --> 00:01:34.560
And that was really
appreciated at the time.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.029
-We like to do that.
-So, you're going back.

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:39.231
This podcast is
you guys recollecting

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:40.466
your time together on the show.

00:01:40.466 --> 00:01:42.334
Obviously, you were both there
the whole time.

00:01:42.334 --> 00:01:44.604
How often do
your memories differ?

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:45.738
-Oh, my gosh.

00:01:45.738 --> 00:01:47.740
Well, when we started
writing this book,

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.541
there was one that
came up right away,

00:01:49.541 --> 00:01:51.744
which was when you --

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:55.815
She got us backstage at
the Emmys, on the actual stage.

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:58.283
-Here's what happened.
-Now, listen, here's the thing.

00:01:58.283 --> 00:01:59.318
She was hungry.

00:01:59.318 --> 00:02:02.321
-I don't like
to be hungry, okay?

00:02:02.321 --> 00:02:04.890
I'm not one of those actresses
that doesn't eat food.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.025
I like to eat the food
and I eat a lot of food

00:02:07.025 --> 00:02:08.194
and I was very hungry.

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:09.428
-Sure.
-Yeah.

00:02:09.428 --> 00:02:11.530
-And we couldn't find food
anywhere, so I said,

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.466
"Angela, come on, we've got to
get something to eat."

00:02:14.466 --> 00:02:15.734
So I found this nice lady,

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:17.470
and she had a headset
and a clipboard.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.105
-Yeah. She walked very fast

00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:20.540
and she kept going,
"shh, shh, shh!"

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:21.741
-Mm-hmm, mm-hmm.

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:23.643
And she took us
to this little, like, tent,

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.544
like, a dimly lit tent.

00:02:25.544 --> 00:02:27.146
-It was not a tent.

00:02:27.146 --> 00:02:30.349
It was a giant curtain on
the Emmys stage.

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:33.286
The, like, Emmy presenters
and people were right there,

00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:34.787
and she was like "Shh!"

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:38.591
-But there was a whole,
like, charcuterie station

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:40.192
and there were these cookies.

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:41.327
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:42.795
And, so,
but we didn't realize --

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:45.731
we were -- I forgot this story
when we were writing the book.

00:02:45.731 --> 00:02:48.134
Angela said, "We have to tell
about that time that

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:50.369
Laurence Fishburne
was giving us stink-eye

00:02:50.369 --> 00:02:53.206
because you were
eating the salami next to him."

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:56.275
-I mean, I vaguely heard him
say something like,

00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:59.412
"Who the 'F' are these folks?"

00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:01.647
And then I said,
"Jenna, wrap it up.

00:03:01.647 --> 00:03:02.948
Shove that salami."

00:03:02.948 --> 00:03:04.851
Oh, wait. That sounded bad.
But you know what I mean.

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:06.218
Like, just --
-Yeah.

00:03:06.218 --> 00:03:07.853
-That's what she said.
-Yes!

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:08.954
-There we go.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:08.954 --> 00:03:12.091
Thematic. Thematic.

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.360
You guys -- But, now,
this is very interesting,

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.264
because I've talked to many of
your former castmates,

00:03:18.264 --> 00:03:22.268
all of whom have said that
they are more recognized from

00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:25.838
"The Office" now than they were
when the show was on NBC.

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:28.407
Like, people have found the show
over the years a great deal.

00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:30.510
Now, yet, it was a hit
when it was on TV.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.344
Did you guys --
when did you realize

00:03:32.344 --> 00:03:35.114
that it was a hit for you guys
when it was airing?

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:36.516
-At the time?
-Yeah.

00:03:36.516 --> 00:03:39.919
-It was -- We were almost
canceled every week

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:42.021
for almost
the first two seasons.

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:43.089
-Yeah.

00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:45.124
-But I remember --
What do you think?

00:03:45.124 --> 00:03:48.227
-The Christmas episode.
-Yes, of Season 2.

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:49.428
-Mm-hmm.

00:03:49.428 --> 00:03:51.664
-But that was because
they put it on the iPods.

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:52.765
-Remember iPods?

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:54.967
-You could download it
on to your iPod,

00:03:54.967 --> 00:03:58.070
and we were
the number-1-downloaded show.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:02.007
So people were not actually
watching us live on television.

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:04.110
-They were watching it
on the iPod.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.480
And we were like, "Well, we
might have a job for a while."

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.849
And I went out
and I bought a Honda, so...

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:13.219
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah. Mm-hmm. I know.

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:14.487
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:14.487 --> 00:04:16.755
That's when we knew, when we saw
Angela in the Honda.

00:04:16.755 --> 00:04:17.990
We're like,
"That show's working."

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:19.626
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:19.626 --> 00:04:20.893
One of the things
you guys write about,

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:23.963
which is fascinating to me,
because, obviously,

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:26.832
everyone in this cast was often
in the background of scenes,

00:04:26.832 --> 00:04:29.034
more than any other show
you could imagine.

00:04:29.034 --> 00:04:32.204
What did you put into
your background acting?

00:04:32.204 --> 00:04:34.907
And did you approach it
the same way?

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:37.943
-Well, I did a lot of
journaling in the background

00:04:37.943 --> 00:04:39.945
and I wrote my grocery lists
in the background,

00:04:39.945 --> 00:04:42.281
because our computers didn't
work for the first two seasons.

00:04:42.281 --> 00:04:45.518
They were props.
-They were just a plastic box.

00:04:45.518 --> 00:04:46.953
-Did they eventually start --

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:48.521
In the third season, did they
give you a working computer?

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:52.592
-Yes. We got Internet.
-It was such a big thing.

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:55.094
They gave us a memo that said
"Today, you have Internet."

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:57.830
I was so excited, I saved it.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.365
-She wanted to put it
in the book,

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:00.733
and I said,
"Angela, I just don't think

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:02.768
that's going to land
for people who weren't there."

00:05:02.768 --> 00:05:04.403
I think it was a "you had to be
there" kind of thing.

00:05:04.403 --> 00:05:05.637
-I don't know.

00:05:05.637 --> 00:05:07.840
I think I understand,
because if you actually

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.009
go back and watch
those episodes with the eye of

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:12.344
knowing you guys are
just staring at plastic boxes,

00:05:12.344 --> 00:05:13.479
that seems, like, really rough.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:14.614
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:14.614 --> 00:05:16.348
-That's like a reality show now.

00:05:16.348 --> 00:05:17.650
It's not -- It's like,

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:19.017
"Who can last the longest
without losing their minds?"

00:05:19.017 --> 00:05:20.119
-Who's gonna tap out?

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:21.254
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:21.254 --> 00:05:22.488
-Now, I want to say, Angela,

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:23.989
I feel like
I'm guilty of this, too,

00:05:23.989 --> 00:05:26.692
which is -- You know, obviously,
the Angela in the show

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:29.028
was somebody who sort of
famously didn't like small talk.

00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:31.964
And I think that because
you look like the character

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:33.632
and you have the same name,
people maybe assume

00:05:33.632 --> 00:05:34.767
that was true of you, and, yet,

00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:36.068
I've always found you
to be lovely.

00:05:36.068 --> 00:05:38.036
Is that what you've found from
other fans, as well?

00:05:38.036 --> 00:05:40.873
-People always think
I'm going to be a grump, right?

00:05:40.873 --> 00:05:43.142
Also, I now have, like,
a resting bitch face.

00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:45.845
After nine years,
my face just goes...

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:47.646
Like, and I don't even know.

00:05:47.646 --> 00:05:49.415
But then, like, someone at
Target will be like,

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:50.916
"I love the show,"
and I'm like, "Thanks so much.

00:05:50.916 --> 00:05:52.118
How are you doing?
How's your day?

00:05:52.118 --> 00:05:53.419
Did you get bangs?

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:55.488
'Cause I feel like that frames
your face so cute."

00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:59.091
And they're literally like,
"I've got to go."

00:05:59.091 --> 00:06:01.661
So, I'm like, "Okay."

00:06:01.661 --> 00:06:06.165
-And you --
your father actually --

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:07.533
Did he have
his own paper company

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:08.601
or work at a paper company?

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:10.002
-My father, Jim --

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:12.605
he worked at Boise Cascade,
and they make paper.

00:06:12.605 --> 00:06:16.108
-Okay. So was he extra-proud
when you got your job

00:06:16.108 --> 00:06:18.010
at a fictional paper company?

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:21.013
-Yeah, you know,
I asked the costume designer

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:23.382
if I could swipe one of
the Dunder Mifflin shirts.

00:06:23.382 --> 00:06:24.450
This was early on,

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.151
before they were part of
the merchandise.

00:06:26.151 --> 00:06:27.987
-Yeah. Before you guys
even had Internet.

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:30.155
-Exactly. And she gave it to me.

00:06:30.155 --> 00:06:31.357
I gave it to him for Christmas.

00:06:31.357 --> 00:06:33.225
And he would wear it,
and people would say,

00:06:33.225 --> 00:06:36.061
"Oh, is that the company you
work for, Dunder Mifflin?"

00:06:36.061 --> 00:06:37.196
And he would say,

00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:40.299
"No, my daughter is
the receptionist there."

00:06:41.434 --> 00:06:43.135
And then
people would be confused.

00:06:43.135 --> 00:06:46.238
And then, as the show got more
popular, you know, then --

00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:48.173
-They had to work
backwards to realize

00:06:48.173 --> 00:06:49.308
he was bragging about you.

00:06:49.308 --> 00:06:50.342
-Yes, exactly.

00:06:50.342 --> 00:06:52.845
-When you rewatch the shows --

00:06:52.845 --> 00:06:54.647
my first half of the question
is for you --

00:06:54.647 --> 00:06:58.884
do you find the sort of
Jim-and-Pam romance

00:06:58.884 --> 00:07:02.121
as sort of uncertain that
the audience did

00:07:02.121 --> 00:07:03.889
as they were watching it unfold?

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:07.560
-Yeah, you know, so, for
our podcast, it's a rewatch,

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.529
and I haven't watched
most episodes

00:07:09.529 --> 00:07:10.663
since they aired originally.

00:07:10.663 --> 00:07:12.864
I always watched them
after they aired,

00:07:12.864 --> 00:07:16.068
but back then, my brain
was so caught up with like,

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:17.670
"Oh, shoot, they cut that scene"

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:19.438
or, "Oh, wow,
that improv made it in.

00:07:19.438 --> 00:07:20.840
That's so cool."

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:23.576
But now I feel like
I'm really watching it as

00:07:23.576 --> 00:07:26.478
an audience member,
and it's very surreal.

00:07:26.478 --> 00:07:28.380
I can't even believe it's me.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:29.815
I can't believe I'm Pam

00:07:29.815 --> 00:07:31.951
and I'm watching
this whole thing happen.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:34.821
And it's like,
I know what's going to happen,

00:07:34.821 --> 00:07:37.923
'cause I shot it,
but I'm still like...

00:07:37.923 --> 00:07:39.992
[ Gasps ]
"...is he gonna tell her?

00:07:39.992 --> 00:07:42.762
Why isn't he telling her?"

00:07:42.762 --> 00:07:44.329
-Well, then
that just speaks to it.

00:07:44.329 --> 00:07:46.531
-Yeah, I really get
the audience perspective

00:07:46.531 --> 00:07:47.933
through this rewatch, for sure.

00:07:47.933 --> 00:07:51.771
-Do people come up to you often
about Angela and Dwight?

00:07:51.771 --> 00:07:54.873
-Here are the things I hear
about "Dwangela."

00:07:54.873 --> 00:07:56.141
-Dwangela.

00:07:56.141 --> 00:07:58.311
-I hear, "Ew!

00:07:59.445 --> 00:08:01.747
Gross.

00:08:01.747 --> 00:08:04.383
Why do we need to see that?"

00:08:04.383 --> 00:08:06.618
-It's very nice that people feel

00:08:06.618 --> 00:08:07.653
that they can be
honest with you.

00:08:07.653 --> 00:08:09.021
-Yes.

00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:11.757
-What has it been like,

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:13.825
this realization
that there's generations of

00:08:13.825 --> 00:08:17.096
new fans who have watched this,
especially on streaming,

00:08:17.096 --> 00:08:19.798
where they're just churning
through it so much faster,

00:08:19.798 --> 00:08:21.800
obviously, than when it
aired over and over again?

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:24.870
Because it is, I think,
a show that has provided people

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:26.105
a lot of comfort over the years.

00:08:26.105 --> 00:08:27.439
And it doesn't get less funny,

00:08:27.439 --> 00:08:28.640
but they feel the same
connection to the characters.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:29.675
What has that felt like?

00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:31.343
Because you must hear it
all the time.

00:08:31.343 --> 00:08:32.711
-It's really cool.

00:08:32.711 --> 00:08:35.781
I mean, it makes me happy
to know that

00:08:35.781 --> 00:08:38.117
parents we meet are now
watching it with their kids,

00:08:38.117 --> 00:08:39.785
and they say, "Sometimes,
it's the only thing

00:08:39.785 --> 00:08:41.987
we do together,
is we watch the show."

00:08:41.987 --> 00:08:44.090
And I had shows
like that growing up.

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:45.891
Jenna and I both
have talked about that.

00:08:45.891 --> 00:08:47.326
And it means a lot to us.

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:48.628
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:08:48.628 --> 00:08:50.663
It's like --
I know that, sometimes,

00:08:50.663 --> 00:08:53.965
we're just on in the background
while people are making dinner,

00:08:53.965 --> 00:08:55.901
just keeping you company.

00:08:55.901 --> 00:09:00.539
It's really cool, because you
can put it on at anytime.

00:09:00.539 --> 00:09:03.108
You don't have to wait for
the next episode.

00:09:03.108 --> 00:09:04.843
It's when you want it.

00:09:04.843 --> 00:09:06.378
-It is a little weird,
though, sometimes.

00:09:06.378 --> 00:09:09.582
Like, I had a kid come up to me,
and he said,

00:09:09.582 --> 00:09:12.951
"You look like the older lady
from 'The Office.'

00:09:12.951 --> 00:09:14.520
Are you the older lady?"

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.389
And I said,
"I am the older lady.

00:09:16.389 --> 00:09:17.422
That's me."

00:09:17.422 --> 00:09:18.491
-"And I've got bad news.

00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:20.593
That was the
younger version of me."

00:09:20.593 --> 00:09:22.361
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:09:22.361 --> 00:09:24.196
-"Hey, are you the old guy from
'Weekend Update'?"

00:09:24.196 --> 00:09:25.564
I'm like, "I'm gonna slap you."

00:09:25.564 --> 00:09:27.633
Yeah.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:09:27.633 --> 00:09:30.969
-Hey, congratulations on
the book.

00:09:30.969 --> 00:09:32.304
The podcast is wonderful.

00:09:32.304 --> 00:09:33.372
And it's so wonderful to see
both of you again,

00:09:33.372 --> 00:09:34.473
especially together.

00:09:34.473 --> 00:09:36.141
-We love you!
-Thank you.

00:09:36.141 --> 00:09:37.343
I love you both, as well.

00:09:37.343 --> 00:09:40.812
You guys, Jenna Fischer
and Angela Kinsey.

00:09:40.812 --> 00:09:43.182
"The Office BFFs"
is on sale now,

00:09:43.182 --> 00:09:45.217
